      en: "The Nyanga River, at approximately 600 kilometers long, is the second largest river in Gabon that flows directly into the Atlantic Ocean. It rises in the southern reaches of the Chaillu Massif and follows a unique path, dipping south into the Republic of the Congo before curving back northwest into southern Gabon. Its course traverses deep valleys and expansive floodplains that support a rich diversity of wildlife. At its mouth near the town of Mayumba, the river forms a complex estuary and lagoon system protected by the Mayumba National Park. The Nyanga region remains relatively sparsely populated, which has helped preserve its high degree of biological integrity. The river is a vital source of fish for local communities and is central to their traditional way of life. Geologically, the Nyanga Valley is distinguished by its significant limestone formations and associated cave systems.",
